WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.550
Here at the Eden Project, in the world’s
largest rainforest in captivity,

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:10.310
one plant has been storing up its energy for
years and years and years,

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:16.599
and it’s just finishing releasing all that in one
disgusting 48-hour period.

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:20.300
This is a corpse flower,
and it smells like death.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.460
It's a bit like a dead rat,
mixed with rotten fish.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:30.580
Some people describe it like a bin that hasn't
been cleaned out for maybe two or three weeks.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.879
It's the smell of decay.

00:00:32.879 --> 00:00:39.159
These plants use flies and beetles the way
many plants use bees: for pollination.

00:00:39.159 --> 00:00:43.059
The smell of death: that's
very attractive to flies and beetles

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:45.339
looking for somewhere to lay eggs.

00:00:45.339 --> 00:00:49.670
These bloom only after it's managed to store
enough energy,

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.889
and that can take anywhere
between 7 and 10 years.

00:00:52.889 --> 00:00:56.350
Titan arums bloom for just 48 hours.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.889
On the first night, it can only receive pollen.

00:00:59.889 --> 00:01:05.309
The second night, it releases pollen.
It cannot self-pollinate.

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:08.400
Over time, evolution will move species towards
strategies

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.810
with the greatest chance of reproductive success.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:15.310
And on the face of it, the corpse flower’s
strategy doesn't seem that good:

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.500
just 48 hours every few years.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.870
I mean, surely a better idea would be reproduce
as often as possible.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:26.720
And there are a lot of plants that do that,
as anyone with hay fever knows.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.010
But there is another possible strategy:

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:33.200
store up energy for years and years and years,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.300
and then burn it all in a couple of nights

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:42.410
to send out a mating call so big that everyone
can smell it for miles.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:46.130
It uses heat to make the smell travel further.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.140
These plants are thermogenic. They create
their own heat.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.460
And they go up to around body temperature.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:58.229
It's able to send its scent way up above the
canopy layer of the rainforest,

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:00.200
where it can catch a breeze.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:06.080
And then insects can smell one of these in
bloom over 3,000 metres away.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.259
It's the same temperature as a dying animal,

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:14.450
and the smell is of rotting flesh.
And -- if you come in here --

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.989
this colour, we normally see in the butchers'.

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:19.849
This is the colour of flesh.

00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:24.870
So by many ways, it's trying to lure in those
flies and beetles,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.230
thinking that they've found a corpse.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.909
Which does just leave us with the question
of the smell.

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:31.999
Now, I got here a few hours too late,

00:02:31.999 --> 00:02:34.579
it's apparently past its worst, but...

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.139
...yeah. Yeah, okay. That's bad! That's...

00:02:44.139 --> 00:02:47.029
Thank you very much to the Eden Project for
inviting me here!

00:02:47.029 --> 00:02:49.469
They’ve got a YouTube channel
you can check out,

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:52.620
and they are open year round in Cornwall,
in the south west of England.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.620
